<TopicPage slug="subjects/medical-laboratory-science/foundational/laboratory-operations-and-safety">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Medical laboratory science is the discipline behind most clinical answers — around 70% of
    medical decisions draw on laboratory results. Its foundation is operational: get the right
    specimen, keep the instruments honest, protect the staff, and build systems that catch errors
    before they become diagnoses.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from specimen to trustworthy result" widget="equationStep" config={qcCfg} />

  <h2>Quality control in practice</h2>
  <p>
    Quality control sounds abstract until you see the Levey–Jennings chart: control values plotted
    run after run, drifting slowly upward as a reagent ages, until a rule fires (Westgard rules —
    for example one point beyond ±3 SD) and the analyser stops accepting results. That quiet
    machinery, repeated millions of times daily worldwide, is why a lab number can be believed at
    all.
  </p>

  <h2>Biosafety: the hierarchy that works</h2>
  <p>
    The lab handles blood and pathogens by definition, so safety is engineered, not hoped for:
    universal precautions (treat every specimen as infectious), containment levels matched to
    organisms, sharps discipline, and ventilation. The classic instruction — never mouth-pipette,
    never recap by hand — each memorialises an accident. Modern biosafety descends from a century
    of them.
  </p>

  <h2>The professions inside the profession</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline is a federation: haematology, chemistry, microbiology, transfusion, molecular —
    each with its own instruments, logic and failure modes (later units take each in turn). What
    unites them is the operational culture: calibration, controls, and the professional habit of
    asking "would I bet this patient's treatment on this number?" That question is the job.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASCP BOC content outline / NAACLS standards; Westgard, <em>Basic QC Practices</em>; CDC/NIOSH biosafety guidance.</p>
</TopicPage>
